{"qid":"Q889","names":["Afghanistan"],"title":"Afghanistan","endonym":{"native":"افغانستان","lang":"ps"},"capsule":"The Democratic Republic of Afghanistan was proclaimed in April 1978 following the Saur Revolution, a coup by the Marxist People's Democratic Party of Afghanistan (PDPA) that overthrew President Daoud Khan; Soviet forces invaded in December 1979, triggering a decade-long guerrilla war by US- and Pakistan-backed mujahideen that killed between 562,000 and two million Afghans and displaced six million. Soviet withdrawal was completed in February 1989, communist governance collapsed in 1992, and the Taliban — emerging from Pakistani religious schools in 1994 — captured Kabul in 1996 and imposed a strict Islamist emirate until a US-led coalition overthrew them in late 2001 following the September 11 attacks. A NATO-backed Islamic Republic governed under the 2004 constitution until the Taliban's rapid reconquest and seizure of Kabul on 15 August 2021 as US forces withdrew, restoring the Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan, which remains unrecognized by most states as of 2025.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Afghanistan's national boundaries are internationally defined; internal territorial control has been radically contested throughout the period, particularly 1992–2001 and 2001–2021, and any cartographic boundary represents a political claim rather than effective administrative reach.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Afghanistan","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afghanistan"},{"label":"Britannica: Afghanistan","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Afghanistan"}],"britannica":"pl